[SPEAKER_02]: It's mental, always mental.

388
00:27:20,176 --> 00:27:27,778
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you observing, observing, there you go, observing sensation in your legs, means you're perceiving sense of touch in your legs.

389
00:27:28,198 --> 00:27:28,418
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

390
00:27:29,058 --> 00:27:33,279
[SPEAKER_02]: That then, somehow, starts to stand for the feeling, vedanā.

391
00:27:34,139 --> 00:27:35,899
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how can you perceive vedanā?

392
00:27:37,619 --> 00:27:38,320
[SPEAKER_02]: How is that possible?

393
00:27:38,740 --> 00:27:40,540
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm perceiving what I'm feeling.

394
00:27:41,682 --> 00:27:47,785
[SPEAKER_02]: But the Buddha said, feeling is that which feels, that it's felt, and perception is that which perceives, that's perceived.

395
00:27:47,885 --> 00:27:49,906
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how can you then perceive what you feel?

396
00:27:50,206 --> 00:27:57,990
[SPEAKER_01]: The sensations are usually described as, like... Well, from the point of view of perception, they're described as feelings.

397
00:27:58,030 --> 00:28:00,071
[SPEAKER_02]: If you look at feelings, they're described as perceptions.

398
00:28:00,551 --> 00:28:00,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

399
00:28:01,211 --> 00:28:06,914
[SPEAKER_02]: And you will maintain that ambiguity, that confusion, that contradiction, because you don't clarify what it is.

400
00:28:06,934 --> 00:28:07,654
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the...

401
00:28:07,774 --> 00:28:09,115
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when people are...

402
00:28:09,955 --> 00:28:12,457
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all fine.

403
00:28:12,517 --> 00:28:14,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody denies the existence of sensations.

404
00:28:28,863 --> 00:28:30,864
[SPEAKER_02]: Or that which you refer to as sensations.

405
00:28:31,564 --> 00:28:38,026
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's the confusion that you imply with it, as if that which you're perceiving is your feeling.

406
00:28:38,826 --> 00:28:53,191
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, all I need to do is stop ignoring how I... well, start ignoring or maintain the ignorance of how I'm actually feeling, and keep hoping for the right perception that will reveal the nature of feeling and everything else to me, because you assume that it can be perceived.

407
00:28:54,436 --> 00:28:55,177
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, you can...

408
00:28:55,277 --> 00:28:59,359
[SPEAKER_02]: I have various sensations in my back, after, you know, a bee stings you.

409
00:29:01,161 --> 00:29:02,421
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's not how I feel.

410
00:29:03,742 --> 00:29:13,429
[SPEAKER_02]: Those are things that I perceive, because the body is spatial, three-dimensional, it has the insides, has the outsides, and, you know, touch receptors go both ways.

411
00:29:14,009 --> 00:29:17,952
[SPEAKER_02]: So you can feel internal sort of perceptions, which are sensations.

412
00:29:18,652 --> 00:29:21,815
[SPEAKER_02]: But then you saying that that's what your feelings are,

413
00:29:23,320 --> 00:29:24,780
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you maintain the wrong view.

414
00:29:26,781 --> 00:29:29,181
[SPEAKER_02]: Because feelings are felt on the level of the mind.

415
00:29:30,302 --> 00:29:32,742
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why we start with, like, what's your mood right now?

416
00:29:33,582 --> 00:29:37,323
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where the feelings are.

417
00:29:37,483 --> 00:29:40,304
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I'm feeling pleasure in my hand.

418
00:29:40,424 --> 00:29:40,584
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

419
00:29:40,624 --> 00:29:44,425
[SPEAKER_02]: You have agreeable perceptions in your hand that result in experience of pleasure.

420
00:29:45,165 --> 00:29:45,545
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

421
00:29:46,985 --> 00:29:49,726
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not how you must stop paying attention to sensations.

422
00:29:53,708 --> 00:29:54,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Feelings in my body.

423
00:29:55,209 --> 00:29:55,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

424
00:29:55,890 --> 00:29:57,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Feelings being in a place.

425
00:29:57,171 --> 00:29:57,351
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

426
00:29:57,591 --> 00:29:59,172
[SPEAKER_02]: Feelings are placed in this body.

427
00:29:59,192 --> 00:29:59,533
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

428
00:30:00,173 --> 00:30:03,716
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then you can measure the feeling by the means of perceptions.

429
00:30:03,976 --> 00:30:04,277
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

430
00:30:05,297 --> 00:30:08,340
[SPEAKER_00]: It was like a surgery I had in my hand.

431
00:30:08,720 --> 00:30:08,840
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh.

432
00:30:09,961 --> 00:30:16,507
[SPEAKER_00]: And they sort of put the area, they numbed it out, so you don't feel anything.

433
00:30:16,847 --> 00:30:17,047
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

434
00:30:22,038 --> 00:30:31,226
[SPEAKER_00]: But they would numb it out, and I could feel them putting on the tendons, which is not something that is agreeable to me.

435
00:30:32,286 --> 00:30:35,849
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I could feel that, and I thought, ah, it's not numb enough, because I feel pain.

436
00:30:37,010 --> 00:30:37,531
[SPEAKER_01]: I feel pain.

437
00:30:38,732 --> 00:30:42,054
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'm sensing only the putting of the tendon, which is disagreeable.

438
00:30:42,495 --> 00:30:43,916
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, they would numb it out even more.

439
00:30:44,056 --> 00:30:45,317
[SPEAKER_00]: And still, the pain was there.

440
00:30:45,337 --> 00:30:47,759
[SPEAKER_00]: And I realized, oh, wait, they can't numb that out.

441
00:30:48,059 --> 00:30:49,040
[SPEAKER_00]: They cannot numb.

442
00:30:53,311 --> 00:30:58,372
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how people experience pain in their limbs after they lost a whole limb.

443
00:30:58,452 --> 00:30:59,772
[SPEAKER_02]: Wrecking, unbearable pain.

444
00:30:59,792 --> 00:31:03,693
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of the feeling, in regard to... That's the thing.

445
00:31:03,973 --> 00:31:06,834
[SPEAKER_02]: Feeling is always phantom, from the point of view of perception.

446
00:31:06,914 --> 00:31:08,694
[SPEAKER_02]: Feeling can never enter the domain of perception.

447
00:31:08,714 --> 00:31:14,315
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have feeling, perception and intention are impossible to disjoin, as the Buddha said.

448
00:31:14,675 --> 00:31:18,356
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whenever there is a perception, there is a simultaneously present enduring feeling.

449
00:31:19,236 --> 00:31:22,357
[SPEAKER_02]: Disagreeable perception, but then some people will find that pleasurable.

450
00:31:23,892 --> 00:31:33,056
[SPEAKER_02]: There are people who find pain being... discomfort, extreme, piercing discomfort being inflicted upon the body, they find pleasure in it.

451
00:31:34,156 --> 00:31:37,878
[SPEAKER_02]: There are other people who have extreme amount of comfort afflicted

452
00:31:41,759 --> 00:31:45,161
[SPEAKER_02]: So, mind determines, basically, the threshold of what's going to be painful or not.

453
00:31:45,802 --> 00:31:50,245
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, the, you know, the trained body, the body resistant to elements, can endure much more.

454
00:31:50,285 --> 00:32:02,614
[SPEAKER_02]: The mind developed in jhānas can endure the roughness of the root of a tree, horrible fermented urine for the medicine, rough hemp robes, will be like silk and basmati rice in the king's palace.

455
00:32:03,295 --> 00:32:04,856
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how it will be felt.

456
00:32:06,057 --> 00:32:10,260
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why Buddha said you should practice jhāna and develop your mind, in that sense, correct sense.

457
00:32:11,631 --> 00:32:26,778
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you think that you can actually perceive your feelings, well, you're completely... it's uncalled for, but you end up giving significance to your perception that doesn't deserve.

458
00:32:28,319 --> 00:32:28,999
[SPEAKER_02]: It shouldn't be there.

459
00:32:29,160 --> 00:32:30,080
[SPEAKER_02]: It's misconceiving it.

460
00:32:31,698 --> 00:32:35,199
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you don't need to deny sensations, or say sensations are wrong.

461
00:32:35,539 --> 00:32:36,680
[SPEAKER_02]: You just need to understand them.

462
00:32:37,320 --> 00:32:43,342
[SPEAKER_02]: And you'll see, if you're either taking them as perceptions that stand in for feelings, or feelings that stand for perceptions, or both.

463
00:32:43,922 --> 00:32:46,502
[SPEAKER_02]: But once you clarify them, you won't do that anymore.

464
00:32:48,163 --> 00:32:50,944
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can still use it as a designation and a form of speech.

465
00:32:51,764 --> 00:32:53,104
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I've got sensations in my leg.

466
00:32:53,124 --> 00:32:54,325
[SPEAKER_02]: I've got to change my posture.

467
00:32:54,485 --> 00:32:54,825
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

468
00:33:06,993 --> 00:33:13,078
[SPEAKER_01]: And you aren't going to find vedanā by focusing?

469
00:33:13,578 --> 00:33:13,718
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

470
00:33:14,239 --> 00:33:16,321
[SPEAKER_02]: Because what you can focus on is what you can observe.

471
00:33:17,041 --> 00:33:19,263
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can't observe feelings, you can only feel them.

472
00:33:21,184 --> 00:33:31,112
[SPEAKER_02]: But if your only means of any form of effort is on the level of focusing, focusing means this whole domain of what's being felt right there simultaneously with it is invisible to you.

473
00:33:32,854 --> 00:33:33,774
[SPEAKER_02]: Knowledge, however,

474
00:33:34,823 --> 00:33:37,625
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a blanket access to all of the domains.

475
00:33:38,585 --> 00:33:48,691
[SPEAKER_02]: Nature of... understanding the nature of things that you're not directly focusing on, but discerning it as a necessary basis, means it's base for understanding intention, feeling, and perception, for that matter.

476
00:33:51,813 --> 00:33:55,555
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, you can use a base of perception to establish anchoring of your mindfulness.

477
00:33:55,595 --> 00:33:56,115
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, for example,

478
00:34:03,067 --> 00:34:09,593
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whatever you do, whatever we say, whatever you feel, whatever... whichever way your body moves, it's done within the container of the light that still endures.

479
00:34:10,173 --> 00:34:10,914
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's not a new.

480
00:34:11,054 --> 00:34:12,075
[SPEAKER_02]: You didn't create this light.

481
00:34:13,116 --> 00:34:17,519
[SPEAKER_02]: Is there a reason they provide a basis for the whole experience on a more particular level that's happening to you?

482
00:34:17,900 --> 00:34:20,642
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's not something I can focus on, because I can't see it.

483
00:34:20,682 --> 00:34:21,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

484
00:34:21,603 --> 00:34:26,687
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you do anchoring properly, you do focusing properly, it's something you don't focus on anymore.

485
00:34:26,967 --> 00:34:29,409
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, focusing means bringing attention to it, but doesn't

486
00:34:39,286 --> 00:34:46,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Even... so, even perceptions aren't really things that I'm attending... attending to.

487
00:34:46,829 --> 00:34:47,889
[SPEAKER_02]: Any of the aggregates for that matter.

488
00:34:47,929 --> 00:34:48,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

489
00:34:48,769 --> 00:34:51,190
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like... That's why the Buddha, as you said in the last talk...

490
00:34:51,210 --> 00:34:54,011
[SPEAKER_01]: I attend to these things, but within that I can...

491
00:34:54,091 --> 00:34:55,712
[SPEAKER_01]: I can discern various perceptions.

492
00:34:55,892 --> 00:34:56,872
[SPEAKER_01]: They're all with peripherals.

493
00:34:56,992 --> 00:35:01,594
[SPEAKER_02]: If you have a view that you must attend and focus, you have a view that prioritizes the content.

494
00:35:05,525 --> 00:35:07,226
[SPEAKER_02]: It has to be done at the expense of the nature.

495
00:35:07,607 --> 00:35:08,347
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what Buddha said.

496
00:35:08,427 --> 00:35:10,409
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever aggregate of perception...

497
00:35:10,609 --> 00:35:22,479
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whatever perception... old, new, past, future, far, near, up, down, left, right... Whatever particularly the perception might be, he knows perception is there.

498
00:35:23,360 --> 00:35:25,261
[SPEAKER_02]: He doesn't go, oh, this is... No.

499
00:35:25,541 --> 00:35:28,003
[SPEAKER_02]: Perception is there because it has to be in some way.

500
00:35:28,444 --> 00:35:31,546
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, you stop being concerned about what way perception is there.

501
00:35:31,606 --> 00:35:32,927
[SPEAKER_02]: You know perception is there.

502
00:35:33,588 --> 00:35:34,569
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever feeling...

503
00:35:35,714 --> 00:35:42,299
[SPEAKER_02]: Past, present, future, far or near, personal, impersonal, pleasant, unpleasant feeling is there.

504
00:35:42,860 --> 00:35:49,425
[SPEAKER_02]: So, every aggregate can be... is present particularly, but if that's all you see, you're ignorant of its nature.

505
00:35:49,845 --> 00:35:51,106
[SPEAKER_02]: Means you're ignorant of the dhamma.

506
00:35:53,368 --> 00:36:00,854
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why focusing outlines the picture for you, but understanding

507
00:36:01,955 --> 00:36:07,038
[SPEAKER_02]: Manasikāra is on the level of the opposite of what you're attending.

508
00:36:07,658 --> 00:36:08,699
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why yoniso.

509
00:36:12,381 --> 00:36:13,042
[SPEAKER_02]: Manasikāra.

510
00:36:13,062 --> 00:36:13,762
[SPEAKER_02]: From the origin.

511
00:36:13,902 --> 00:36:14,723
[SPEAKER_02]: From the origin.

512
00:36:14,903 --> 00:36:23,648
[SPEAKER_02]: So, knowing the origin of this very thing that I'm attending, while this origin of that attention is present.

513
00:36:23,968 --> 00:36:26,410
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you attend the origin of your attention?

514
00:36:27,130 --> 00:36:27,951
[SPEAKER_02]: You won't attend it.

515
00:36:28,391 --> 00:36:28,631
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

516
00:36:34,338 --> 00:36:45,148
[SPEAKER_02]: That is inconceivable, but I will know that there is that because of which I'm able to see that I cannot see, but I can know that it's there as a necessary basis, namely the I.

517
00:36:46,169 --> 00:36:49,372
[SPEAKER_02]: That's properly exercised yoniso manasikāra.

518
00:36:54,196 --> 00:36:54,917
[SPEAKER_02]: That's all in the suttas.

519
00:37:03,538 --> 00:37:11,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I guess most people would think yoniso means attending carefully, attending... that kind of thing.

520
00:37:11,901 --> 00:37:17,183
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you can attend it carefully on the level of nature of things, not on the level of what you can, like, observe and stare.

521
00:37:17,203 --> 00:37:17,423
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

522
00:37:18,303 --> 00:37:23,845
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if it's the origin of my attention, how can that same attention attend itself in its origin?

523
00:37:27,047 --> 00:37:27,547
[SPEAKER_02]: Impossible.

524
00:37:28,687 --> 00:37:31,028
[SPEAKER_02]: And the only reason you would keep assuming that is because

525
00:37:41,200 --> 00:37:42,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Context of potential.

526
00:37:42,981 --> 00:37:43,801
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, pretty much.

527
00:37:45,162 --> 00:37:46,083
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I mean.

528
00:37:46,263 --> 00:37:48,044
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you attend something from nothingness?

529
00:37:48,164 --> 00:37:50,425
[SPEAKER_02]: No, but it needs to be there, beforehand.

530
00:37:50,905 --> 00:37:59,230
[SPEAKER_01]: And that context is the origin of this thing, in a sense that this thing couldn't be what it is without this whole larger context being there.

531
00:37:59,250 --> 00:38:00,931
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, attend to your elbows touching the chair.

532
00:38:01,151 --> 00:38:03,212
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you do that if you wouldn't have elbows?

533
00:38:03,312 --> 00:38:03,952
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no chair.

534
00:38:03,972 --> 00:38:04,793
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're not sitting and

535
00:38:12,500 --> 00:38:13,881
[SPEAKER_02]: If it's already there, enduring.

536
00:38:15,722 --> 00:38:20,084
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you attend to any aspect, it's not new.

537
00:38:20,565 --> 00:38:21,025
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

538
00:38:21,185 --> 00:38:22,045
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not begun.

539
00:38:22,266 --> 00:38:23,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

540
00:38:23,406 --> 00:38:24,847
[SPEAKER_00]: It's been going on and on and on.

541
00:38:24,867 --> 00:38:25,968
[SPEAKER_02]: It's there, enduring.

542
00:38:26,028 --> 00:38:33,652
[SPEAKER_02]: Fully understand that, that's how you also become unable to regard the most volitional choice, decision, as mine.

543
00:38:34,372 --> 00:38:38,795
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you see it fully encapsulated within that which cannot be yours.

544
00:38:38,815 --> 00:38:40,436
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you can only choose what's already there.

545
00:38:40,456 --> 00:38:40,696
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

546
00:38:41,450 --> 00:38:45,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the option to choose a certain choice is there.

547
00:38:45,891 --> 00:38:47,791
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you choose it.

548
00:38:48,111 --> 00:38:49,211
[SPEAKER_00]: That choice has always been there.

549
00:38:49,351 --> 00:38:49,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

550
00:38:49,952 --> 00:38:51,152
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, or you can even look.

551
00:38:51,952 --> 00:38:54,652
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm still sitting here.

552
00:38:55,292 --> 00:38:58,273
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm still... the choice to sit here is still going on.

553
00:38:58,673 --> 00:39:02,914
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you say now, oh, but I have a choice to stand up and change that situation.

554
00:39:02,994 --> 00:39:03,234
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

555
00:39:03,334 --> 00:39:09,315
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can only do that because there is a possibility for you, as in for the body, to be moved in an upright position.

556
00:39:09,975 --> 00:39:16,497
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you... when you stand up, you just exercise another choice on top of the choice that's been exercised of you sitting.

557
00:39:17,297 --> 00:39:32,741
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there's no amount of... there is no such choice that can take you outside of the given container of your experience as a whole, from which you can really impartially or independently or like as a master choose without any basis for that choice.

558
00:39:33,441 --> 00:39:33,722
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

559
00:39:33,882 --> 00:39:35,142
[SPEAKER_02]: It's always secondary to it.

560
00:39:36,042 --> 00:39:38,583
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the choice... the effects of your choice

561
00:39:40,267 --> 00:39:44,849
[SPEAKER_02]: The results of your action are within your experience as a whole.

562
00:39:44,869 --> 00:39:45,869
[SPEAKER_02]: They're secondary to it.

563
00:39:46,109 --> 00:39:54,792
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if I choose to stand up, I've changed my initially determined sort of base for my mindfulness that I focused on initially to just discern it.

564
00:39:55,872 --> 00:40:01,234
[SPEAKER_02]: But now, I can only change it because there is another potential basis that this can be replaced with.

565
00:40:01,454 --> 00:40:02,995
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, the four body postures.

566
00:40:03,335 --> 00:40:04,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there's no fifth, by the way.

567
00:40:05,716 --> 00:40:09,077
[SPEAKER_02]: You're either going to be standing, laying down, walking or sitting.

568
00:40:11,691 --> 00:40:12,051
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

569
00:40:13,172 --> 00:40:20,156
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's when you start realizing that it doesn't matter how much I exercise my choice within this, it's going to be within some limiting container.

570
00:40:20,616 --> 00:40:23,898
[SPEAKER_02]: Fundamentally, that limiting container is the five aggregates or the life in itself.

571
00:40:24,258 --> 00:40:26,299
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't do anything unless you're alive.

572
00:40:27,940 --> 00:40:28,781
[SPEAKER_02]: So, am I still alive?

573
00:40:28,821 --> 00:40:29,001
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

574
00:40:29,381 --> 00:40:31,222
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's your... the most general container.

575
00:40:34,744 --> 00:40:36,525
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if that's taken away, which can be at any

576
00:40:43,939 --> 00:40:46,840
[SPEAKER_02]: And if that goes, none of this can remain.

577
00:40:46,980 --> 00:40:51,582
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you keep that context in your mind, that's basically maraṇasati, done correctly.

578
00:40:51,642 --> 00:40:54,864
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything is then put against the backdrop of

579
